A||N||6||Red2m4||My fiero caught fire!||||9||
Z||000000||Red2m4||05-06-2000||02:48 PM||||Whaaaaaaaaaahhh!!!!!<BR> My 2m4 caught fire this afternoon, anybody tell me why? (It's an '86, so It shouldn't be of the year prone to fire damage, correct?)  Here what happend, I bought it in Jan, didnt use it till April. I'm driving down the expressway, decide to test the air conditioner, worked fine, shut it off.  Got off the express way, after waiting at a light at the exit, there was a pop and a jolt as I took off, "wierd" I thought, especially since I just replaced the dog bone.  Driving to work, It was getting really crappy acceleration, then as I pulled in to a parking lot, the brakes were close to gone. I stop the car, look at it, flames are shooting out from the rear driverside tire.  Got an extinguisher, put it out.<P>   1. The fire had a nasty rubber smell to it.<P>  2.  The first thing I noticed was a problem with the brakes. <P>  3. It was the first time I had used the AC.<P>   4. I ccouldnt quite tell, but the "brake" light on the dash was on, I thought, hard to tell though as it was such a sunny day.  Tried the parking brake, and that was fine.<P>   It's a 4 cyl auto, 43k mi.<P>    I like the car, want to keep it, any one knows what went wrong, let me know.  ||||reg||9
Z||000001||LarryB||05-06-2000||06:14 PM||||Sounds like you drove the car with the parking brake set or you had something happen like the caliper freezing closed. In either case driving with the brakes dragging generates a tremendous amount of heat from the friction and some of that heat is going to get transferred through the metal to the wheel.  <P>That would be consistent with the crappy acceleration as well.<BR> <P>||||reg||1
Z||000002||RiceCooker||05-06-2000||10:05 PM||||Very Sorry to hear of your misfortune<BR>A Moment of Silence Please||||reg||9
Z||000003||Shiner||05-06-2000||10:51 PM||||Hey!  From what you were describing, it sounds to me like your A/C compressor freezed up.  The belt burning would make that rubber smell.  What have you done to the car since it caught fire?||||reg||1
Z||000004||geyser||05-06-2000||11:02 PM||||Look closely at the burned area. Try to find the area that has the worst or deepest burn. All fires leave a path of travel. The area of origin will have the deepest burn. An example would be- if the brakes were the culprit, the fire would have started from the wheel area and traveled up into the wheel well/body. If it were an engine compartment fire the origin would have the worst burn and then travel upward/outward from there. The parking brake left on could indeed start a fire. An engine fire in an 86 would most likely have started from an electrical problem. Let me know if this helps.||||reg||1
Z||000005||Racer 1||05-06-2000||11:26 PM||||Maybe your fiero didnt like you? So were you still in your burning car inspecting the brake handle? Post some pics.||||reg||5
Z||000006||Red2m4||05-07-2000||03:24 AM||||<BR>  I managed to extinguish it pretty fast, there was no damage to the body, that I could see.  I drove the car to my mechanic (only 100 yards away, at 12 am.) Would a siezed caliper trigger the brake light? I did take pic's, maybe I'll post them when I get the time.||||reg||5
